#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421784 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3uv,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-3u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩|}_{x=3uv\\,y=u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨3(3uv),\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨9uv,\\,-{u}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,3v,\\,-3u⟩ \\\\  & =9uv\\cdot (-1)+(-{u}^{2})\\cdot 3v+2\\cdot (-3u) \\\\  & =-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\,-{y}^{2},\\,2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-9uv-3{u}^{2}v-6u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}v-{u}^{3}v-3{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{11}{2}v-3)\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{11}{4}{v}^{2}-3v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{23}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421785 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩|}_{x=-v\\,y=-u\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,-2(-v),\\,-(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 0+2v\\cdot (-2u)+u\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4uv+u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{u}^{2}v+\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2v+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{[{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423051 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${e}^{{x}^{2}y}={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}y} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}+9 \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}+3{x}^{2})i+({x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-2y)j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =(2(2)(0){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}+3({2}^{2}))i+(({2}^{2}){e}^{({2}^{2})(0)}-2(0))j \\\\  & =12\\,i+4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨12,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨12,4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{12} & =\\frac{y-0}{4} \\\\ 4(x-2) & =12y \\\\ x-2 & =3y \\\\ x-3y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423120 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-x-y$ for $-2\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=-u-v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot (1)+({v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2})\\cdot (1)+(-u-v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2}+(-u-v)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\color{blue}{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423121 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z={x}^{2}y$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}xy+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z={x}^{2}y,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}y⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2xy⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2xy \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-2P}\\hspace{0.167em}xy+(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423122 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂y}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂x}}-Q\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂y}+{\\color{blue}R})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423123 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-3\\le y\\le 0.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-3\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1+(u-v)^{2}\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\color{blue}5uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423124 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-3y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-3v} \\\\  & =⟨u+u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2(u-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}v+u-3v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(2u-3v)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-6v)\\cdot (3)+(u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+3v+6u-18v+u-2v \\\\  & =5u-17v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-17v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\color{blue}(5u-17v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424097 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-4,1)$ and $B(0,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(0,-2)-f(-4,1) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}-2(-2)^{3})-((-4)^{2}-2(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16-16+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424178 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(0-1)+(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424179 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =a & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =b & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(bc-0) \\\\  & =abc.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424180 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u}+v,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(v-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{u}(v-w) & =0 \\\\ v-w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-w=0$ defines the plane $v=w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424181 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y},\\,v(x,y,z)=y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z(0-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{y} & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424182 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(6-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424190 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x-y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2(x-y)\\,i-2(x-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\,i-2\\cdot (2-1)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =2\\cdot (1-2)\\,i-2\\cdot (1-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,i-2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,j=4\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424226 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227 :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $∇\\cdot F>0$ is true, whereas statements $∇\\cdot F=0$ and $∇\\cdot F<0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∇\\cdot F>0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $∇\\cdot F<0$ is true, whereas statements $∇\\cdot F=0$ and $∇\\cdot F>0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∇\\cdot F<0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424229 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x+y)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424329 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a51719e208dafaa32d7f28c338885313942513493bd9fe0392b1ef06004e91e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/43/4371a691b81b2ed1cb5d3468f68cd24f41e8a6b87d378a3206238d58e61791f0.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x+y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424330 :question/problem "![](images/44/44b6b5826b5d8532fef3a9c8e56a03b3244a4aaa33ae00b4b12b69364c51f001.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the right-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331 :question/problem "![](images/31/31054ef4614814dd1c9131192504ea37f3527af75d8292fbce943c16235c9303.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ \nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424514 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0bf7b88eadd199edc1997410b7ddbaa74b204e7d0f8afef7c9b17c28d8f8dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{x}^{2}-4yx]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({1}^{2}-4y(1))-((-1)^{2}-4y(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-8y\\,dy \\\\  & =-4{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-4(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424515 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨14xy,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=14xy$ and $Q(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}(14y+6{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{y}^{2}+6{x}^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{x}^{6}+6{x}^{5})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{7}+{x}^{6})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424516 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\hspace{0.222em}xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424518 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7400787e09600d370fa5c554e616fad6ba3a126a09725cfb236502f81a7e70e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4xy-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(-4(1)^{2}y-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4y-6{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[-2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3})-(-2(-1)^{2}-2(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424519 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,-1),(1,-1),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(6xy+y{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/86/861b873005c03ea6c25255f2febc975ff153d939780ec46e4f4503cbb50640dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6xy+y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6y+2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6y+2xy)+(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y(2+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424520 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/72/728036ffc90e2476017b3102b6279f9c5900f0e7fc58536e76c9aa038eb04f02.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y+1\\le x\\le {e}^{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}+y-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{[{e}^{y}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[(e+\\frac{1}{2}-1)-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424521 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/0b/0b79306d0e78da924aa31b3bf1c73b7430ca322abc7f5a9b3107bb0a4d662182.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3-{x}^{2}y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424522 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/63/636bfd79297e6fef4b0e56af9b0cb22f2e9a8061e833ea1dcf07e7958b870cdc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}+5)y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=5({x}^{3}+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424524 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9472021d3973167a7601785803837627476a4e8b1460e42f87e91ecb99d7e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620 :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425118 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
